{"stats":{"took":99,"total":17,"estimate":false,"maxScore":174.14937},"results":[{"_meta":{"score":174.14937},"keywords":[],"created":"2026-11-14T06:55:57.989192+00:00","distributionDate":"2026-11-13T06:55:57.989113+00:00","fundingNumbers":[{"number":"80NSSC20M0119","type":"funding"}],"distribution":"PUBLIC","stiType":"ACCEPTED_MANUSCRIPT","title":"Burning in microgravity: Experimental results and analysis","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Parham Dehghani"},"organization":{"name":""}}},{"sequence":1,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Peter B. Sunderland"},"organization":{"name":""}}},{"sequence":2,"meta":{"author":{"name":"James G. Quintiere"},"organization":{"name":""}}},{"sequence":3,"meta":{"author":{"name":"John L. deRis"},"organization":{"name":""}}}],"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","stiTypeDetails":"Accepted Manuscript","modified":"2026-06-11T00:00:00","id":"27482090396921","sourceIdentifiers":[{"number":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.combustflame.2021.01.035"}],"publications":[{"volume":"228","pages":"315-330","issn":"0010-2180","publisher":"Elsevier BV","publicationName":"Combustion and Flame","issuePublicationDate":"2021-06-01T00:00:00","doi":"10.1016/j.combustflame.2021.01.035"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"chorus-2026-11-14-07-03"},{"_meta":{"score":157.83333},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"GOV_PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20020024460,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"9a3b2761cb58495d97ff4f57388cf209","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Inorganic, Organic And Physical Chemistry"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20020024460,"ear":"NO","id":"abd94b3064f94d49b28f9035fd903c84","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","fundingNumbers":[{"number":"RTOP 101-32-0D","submissionId":20020024460,"id":"e174f2882ecc457e8e8c1249e6e0b26b","type":"PROJECT"},{"number":"NCC3-655","submissionId":20020024460,"id":"d978ed83be874e518bca511dcfb244f4","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"}],"title":"Experimental and Modeling Study of the Burning of an Ethanol Droplet in Microgravity","stiType":"PREPRINT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-09-07T12:40:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20020024460,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Kazakov, Andrei"},"organization":{"name":"Princeton Univ.","location":"NJ United States"}},"id":"69fb2bdac62041fcacf8989c09031bb2"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20020024460,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Conley, Jordan"},"organization":{"name":"Princeton Univ.","location":"NJ United States"}},"id":"63e0b7eb02d041918e831b2e4500da1a"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20020024460,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Dryer, Frederick L."},"organization":{"name":"Princeton Univ.","location":"NJ United States"}},"id":"901931d50c0a487ca228a717e099580a"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":20020024460,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Ferkul, Paul"},"organization":{}},"id":"21d3512d1cb8436d8eded9a11f7182fd"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Preprint (Draft being sent to journal)","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20020024460,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-09-07T12:40:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"The microgravity ethanol droplet combustion experiments were performed aboard the STS-94/MSL-1 Shuttle mission within the Fiber-Supported Droplet Combustion-2 (FSDC-2) program. The burning histories and flame standoffs for pure ethanol and ethanol/water droplets were obtained from the images recorded with two 8 mm videocameras. The obtained results show that average gasification rate is related to the initial droplet size in a manner similar to n-alkanes and methanol and consistent with the results of Hara and Kumagai and the data taken recently in the NASA-Lewis 2.2 s droptower. A transient, moving finite-element chemically reacting flow model applied previously to sphero-symmetric combustion of methanol, methanol/water, n-alkane, and n-alkane binary mixture droplets was adopted for the problem of ethanol droplet combustion. The model includes detailed description of gas-phase reaction chemistry and transport, a simplified description of liquid phase transport, and non-luminous radiative heat transfer. Gas-phase chemistry was described with the detailed reaction mechanism of Norton and Dryer, which consists of 142 reversible elementary reactions of 33 species. Another recently published reaction mechanism of high-temperature ethanol oxidation was also considered. The model predictions were found to compare favorably with the experimental data. The model analysis also indicates that water condensation in the case of ethanol has smaller effect on average droplet gasification rate as compared with previously studied methanol cases. This effect is explained by non-ideal (azeotropic) behavior of binary ethanol-water mixtures. Further analysis of computational results and ethanol droplet radiative extinction behavior will be discussed.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"submissionId":20020024460,"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH United States","id":"1ff93c45eda24d14a79d74991fee140d"},{"country":"United Kingdom","submissionId":20020024460,"sponsors":[{"organizationId":"60bfc8d085f64ed8b1862ea2a89f89e1","meta":{"organization":{"name":"Princeton Univ.","location":"NJ United States"}},"meetingId":"ced6290cf8a741368d444dfee932349e","id":"4e84efb325a243d9b18d39681fdcce55"},{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH United States"}},"meetingId":"ced6290cf8a741368d444dfee932349e","id":"649597d1d5b0462eb0c3740b7c3d8231"}],"name":"Twenty-Eighth International Symposium on Combustion","location":"Edinburgh","id":"ced6290cf8a741368d444dfee932349e","startDate":"2000-08-02T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20020024460,"id":"aa01440bd4454e27b621a33a9c955596","publicationDate":"2000-01-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","redistributedDate":"2021-08-27T00:00:00.0000000","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"20020024460.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20020024460/downloads/20020024460.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20020024460/downloads/20020024460.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20020024460/downloads/20020024460.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":141.89186},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"GOV_PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20040053529,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"864d46eead694fca93b9e80aa3397baa","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Spacecraft Propulsion And Power"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20040053529,"ear":"NO","id":"5fd392c9a5344a33a69e0103f08e5433","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2018-06-05T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","title":"Combustion Of Interacting Droplet Arrays In Microgravity","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PAPER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-09-07T11:35:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20040053529,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Dietrich, D. L."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH, United States"}},"id":"4e630ae8610c4e74b4a2aec4c80fd549"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20040053529,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Struk, P. M."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Glenn Research Center","location":"Cleveland, OH, United States"}},"id":"e7c5f18f60924a458a5b3ab180e83953"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20040053529,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Ikegami, M."},"organization":{"name":"Agency of Industrial Science and Technology"}},"id":"0e384d3f6bf9451387a2d66ac31f5a46"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":20040053529,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Xu, G."},"organization":{"name":"Agency of Industrial Science and Technology"}},"id":"3dd1013000804025a541b2e18dfea0ba"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20040053529,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-09-07T11:35:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"GRC","name":"Glenn Research Center","id":"19eeb25fa404421d9eaa5f876e853e3c"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Theory and experiments involving single droplet combustion date back to 1953, with the first microgravity work appearing in 1956. The problem of a spherical droplet burning in an infinite, quiescent microgravity environment is a classical problem in combustion research with the classical solution appearing in nearly every textbook on combustion. The microgravity environment offered by ground-based facilities such as drop towers and space-based facilities is ideal for studying the problem experimentally. A recent review by Choi and Dryer shows significant advances in droplet combustion have been made by studying the problem experimentally in microgravity and comparing the results to one dimensional theoretical and numerical treatments of the problem. Studying small numbers of interacting droplets in a well-controlled geometry represents a logical step in extending single droplet investigations to more practical spray configurations. Studies of droplet interactions date back to Rex and co-workers, and were recently summarized by Annamalai and Ryan. All previous studies determined the change in the burning rate constant, k, or the flame characteristics as a result of interactions. There exists almost no information on how droplet interactions a effect extinction limits, and if the extinction limits change if the array is in the diffusive or the radiative extinction regime. Thus, this study examined experimentally the effect that droplet interactions have on the extinction process by investigating the simplest array configuration, a binary droplet array. The studies were both in normal gravity, reduced pressure ambients and microgravity facilities. The microgravity facilities were the 2.2 and 5.2 second drop towers at the NASA Glenn Research Center and the 10 second drop tower at the Japan Microgravity Center. The experimental apparatus and the data analysis techniques are discussed in detail elsewhere.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","publications":[{"submissionId":20040053529,"id":"baf2fd455be34ca488e65cb3daa16a8d","publicationName":"Seventh International Workshop on Microgravity Combustion and Chemically Reacting Systems","publicationDate":"2003-08-01T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[{"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","id":20040053504,"type":"ANALYTIC_PRIMARY","title":"Seventh International Workshop on Microgravity Combustion and Chemically Reacting Systems","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PROCEEDINGS","distribution":"PUBLIC","status":"CURATED"},{"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","id":20040053504,"type":"ANALYTIC_PRIMARY","title":"Seventh International Workshop on Microgravity Combustion and Chemically Reacting Systems","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PROCEEDINGS","distribution":"PUBLIC","status":"CURATED"}],"downloads":[{"dra